<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Japan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Japanese</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Japan is an island nation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. It absorbed many influences from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> and resisted the invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, was unified under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>, and traded with Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchants. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>, which defeated the Qing and Russian empires and attacked British and Dutch possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/japan.jpg" alt="Japan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Various · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Japan is an island country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, made up of 14,125 islands that stretch over 3,000 kilometres along the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The five main islands, from north to south, are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and Okinawa. About 75% of the terrain is mountainous and heavily forested, so the population is concentrated on the coastal plains. Japan lies along the Pacific Ring of Fire and is prone to earthquakes, tsunami and volcanic eruptions; the 1923 Tokyo earthquake killed over 140,000 people, and the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku</a> earthquake triggered a large tsunami.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first known habitation dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-paleolithic">Upper Paleolithic</a>. The Jōmon period began around 14,500 BCE, and the Yayoi people later entered the archipelago from the Korean Peninsula, bringing wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming and metallurgy. According to legend, Emperor Jimmu founded a kingdom in central Japan in 660 BCE. Japan first appears in written history in the Chinese Book of Han, completed in 111 CE. Buddhism was introduced from Baekje in 552, and in 645 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taika-reform">Taika Reforms</a> aimed at greater centralisation of power in the imperial court, on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>From the 12th century actual power was held by military aristocrats known as shōgun and feudal lords called daimyō. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and Ashikaga shogunates and a century of warring states, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> unified the country in 1600 and implemented an isolationist foreign policy. An American fleet forced Japan to open trade in 1853, which led to the end of the shogunate and the restoration of imperial power in 1868. Japan annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1895 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1910, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1937 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> power in 1941. It surrendered in 1945 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> and was under Allied occupation until 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Japan has a population of nearly 123 million, and Tokyo is the capital and largest city. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> in which the emperor's role is ceremonial, and its legislature is the bicameral National Diet. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan">Constitution of Japan</a> was adopted in 1947. Minority groups include the indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Ryukyuan people</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Japan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Argentina remained neutral in both world wars until it declared war on Germany and <b>Japan</b> in March 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Japan</b> was divided between a Northern Court in Kyoto under Ashikaga influence and a Southern Court in Yoshino, the beginning of the Nanboku-chō period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Deerskins went in quantity to <b>Japan</b>, while regular tribute missions to the Ming and later the Qing courts doubled as trading voyages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Sea routes linked Baekje with the southern Chinese courts, the Gaya states and the Japanese archipelago, bringing silks, ceramics and books from China and carrying Baekje goods, scholars and artisans to <b>Japan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tang Xuanming calendar, which <b>Japan</b> used for more than 800 years from 862, first reached Japan with an embassy from Balhae.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Japan</b>'s rapid conquest of Hong Kong, Malaya, Singapore and Burma in 1941–1942, the fall of Singapore in February 1942 above all, destroyed the myth of British invincibility in Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
